  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 102 AM CDT Sun Sep 27 2026\n\nAs of 1 AM CDT, we have some fog moving down out of ND into the \nnorthern portion of the CWA. So far there are only some sparse areas \nof reduced visibilities, but we will keep an eye on this through the \nmorning. Temperatures around the region are in the upper 50s to mid \n60s with light winds out of the southeast. Today will be dry during \nthe daylight hours and the first portion of the overnight hours, \nwith the exception of a slight chance for some showers and weak \nthunderstorms over central SD.\n\nOur next chance for rain moves west to east across the region \nstarting late tonight into early Monday morning and is expected to \nlast into Tuesday afternoon. Chances for more than a half an inch \nhave come down slightly, now between 55 and 75% between Monday and \nTuesday afternoons, mainly along the ND/SD border and along and east \nof US 281. Chances for higher amounts have decreased rather \nsignificantly, now there just a very small area along the ND/SD \nborder of 50-60% chance of more than an inch through Tuesday morning \nwhereas 24 hours ago it was a 55-70% chance over a much larger area. \nThis seems to be a result of the track of the low moving slightly \nnorth. Throughout this event, there is potential for thunderstorms, \nalthough no severe weather is expected.\n\nThe temperature forecast through the period remains relatively \nunchanged with highs right around normal and a slight cooldown to \nend the work week. Winds Monday afternoon with the incoming low \npressure will gust 25-35 mph west of the Prairie Coteau/Sisseton \nHills, highest in south central SD. This looks to last well into the \nevening. Some fog will be possible around I-29 Monday morning.",
